        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Start with the water test, because a faded penetrating stain that already lets water soak in needs cleaning, not stripping. Stripping is genuinely required when a film is peeling, when the old finish still beads water, when absorption varies across the deck, or when you are moving from solid to a more transparent finish.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            When you do strip, the technique is mostly patience. Work in shade in sections you can keep wet, honour the dwell time, agitate rather than attack, and answer a stubborn patch with a second application instead of a pressure washer. Then neutralise with an oxalic acid brightener, which is chemistry rather than cosmetics.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Afterwards, expect a light sand at 60 to 80 grit and a long drying period — two days minimum, more in cool or humid weather, because the boards have been soaked repeatedly. And if the deck carries layers of old solid stain or paint, seriously consider recoating solid instead. That is a legitimate choice, not a surrender.
          </p>
        </div>


